<article class="env-lemma" id="0B56AD">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0B56AD">Lemma <span data-tag="0B56AD">6.1.3.13</span>.</a> Let $\mathbf{A}$ be a simplicial model category containing an object $Z$ which is both fibrant and cofibrant, and let $\mathbf{A}_{/Z}$ be endowed with the induced model structure. Then the natural map $\theta : \sNerve ( \mathbf{A}_{/Z}^{\circ } ) \rightarrow \sNerve (\mathbf{A}^{\circ })_{/Z}$ is an equivalence of $\infty $-categories. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\phi : Z' \rightarrow Z$ be an object of $\sNerve (\mathbf{A}^{\circ })_{/Z}$. Then we can choose a factorization 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  Z' \stackrel{i}{\rightarrow } Z'' \stackrel{\psi }{\rightarrow } Z \]
</div>
    <p>
       where $i$ is a trivial cofibration and $\psi $ is a fibration, corresponding to a fibrant-cofibrant object of $\mathbf{A}_{/Z}$. The above diagram classifies an equivalence between $\phi $ and $\psi $ in $\sNerve (\mathbf{A}^{\circ })_{/Z}$, so that $\theta $ is essentially surjective. 
    <p>
      Recall that for any simplicial category $\calC $ containing a pair of objects $X$ and $Y$, there is a natural isomorphism of simplicial sets 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \Hom ^{\rght }_{\sNerve (\calC )}( X, Y ) \simeq \Sing _{Q^{\bullet }}( \bHom _{\calC }(X,Y) ), \]
</div>
    <p>
       where $Q^{\bullet }$ is the cosimplicial object of $\sSet $ introduced in §<a href="/tag/1F41EE" data-tag="1F41EE">2.2.2</a>. The same calculation shows that if $\phi : X \rightarrow Z$, $\psi : Y \rightarrow Z$ are two morphisms in $\calC $, then 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \Hom ^{\rght }_{ \sNerve (\calC )_{/Z} }(\phi , \psi ) \simeq \Sing _{Q^{\bullet }}(P), \]
</div>
    <p>
       where $P$ denotes the path space 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \bHom _{\calC }(X,Y) \times _{ \bHom _{\calC }(X,Z)^{ \{ 0\}  } } \bHom _{\calC }(X,Z)^{\Delta ^1} \times _{ \bHom _{\calC }(X,Z)^{ \{ 1\}  }} \{  \phi \} . \]
</div>
    <p>
       If $\calC $ is fibrant, then we may identify $M$ with the homotopy fiber of the map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  f: \bHom _{\calC }(X,Y) \stackrel{\psi }{\rightarrow } \bHom _{\calC }(X,Z) \]
</div>
    <p>
       over the vertex $\phi $. Consequently, we may identify the natural map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \Hom ^{\rght }_{ \sNerve ( \calC _{/Z} )}(\phi , \psi ) \rightarrow \Hom ^{\rght }_{ \sNerve (\calC )_{/Z} }(\phi , \psi ) \]
</div>
    <p>
       with $\Sing _{Q^{\bullet }}( \theta )$, where $\theta $ denotes the inclusion of the fiber of $f$ into the homotopy fiber of $f$. Consequently, to show that $\Sing _{Q^{\bullet }}(\theta )$ is a homotopy equivalence, it suffices to prove that $f$ is a Kan fibration. In the special case where $\calC = \mathbf{A}^{\circ }$ and $\psi $ is a fibration, this follows from the definition of a simplicial model category. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>